        "question_id": "e10bio073m00426",
        "question": "What are the advantages of sexual reproduction over asexual reproduction ?",
        "options": [],
        "answer": "na",
        "solution": "1. In asexual reproduction, offspring are almost identical to their parent because they have the same genes as their parent.\r\n2. So, much genetic variation is not possible in asexual reproduction. This is a disadvantage because it inhibits the further evolution of the organism. \r\n3. In sexual reproduction the offspring, although similar to their parents, are not identical to them or to one another.\r\n4. This is because the offspring receive some genes from the mother and some from the father.\r\n5. Because of the mixing of genes of mother and father in various different combinations, all of the offsprings have genetic variations.\r\n6. In this way, sexual reproduction leads to a greater variety in population.\r\n7. This means that a species (animal or plant) can adapt quickly to changes in its surroundings.\r\n8. This is because there are always likely to be some individuals which are more suited to the changes than others, and these individuals will survive and reproduce themselves.",
        "marks": 3,
        "subject_id": "e10bio",
        "medium": "eng",
        "board": "gseb",
        "chapter": "[7] How do Organisms Reproduce ?",
